 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Звонок из Милана  (A Call from Milan)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "angelina_h": "Ангелина",
  "max": "Макс",
  "vika": "Вика"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max realises he's caused a mess, and he has an idea to fix it: a fake phone call. He rings the restaurant pretending to be an Italian and tells the hostess the trade show is cancelled. Russian first, as always.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Бонжорно, синьоры.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Добрый день, ресторан Клод Моне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Меня зовут Александр.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Выставка отменяется.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Передайте это Виктории.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Хорошо, я передам.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Граци, синьора, чао-чао.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Да, алло. Виктория Сергеевна, здравствуйте.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Звонил представитель выставки из Милана.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Он сказал, что она отменяется.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А, правда? Я уже в Домодедово.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я чуть не улетела.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо, что предупредили.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Again, and watch the little acting job.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Бонжорно, синьоры.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Buongiorno, signori.' These are Italian words written in Russian letters, so Max's fake accent does the talking. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Бонжорно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " sounds not quite right, which is the joke.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Добрый день, ресторан Клод Моне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good afternoon, Claude Monet restaurant.' Let's inspect this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Добрый",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "день",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'good day', and Russians use it for the afternoon, too. The name of the restaurant doesn't change, whatever the sentence.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Меня зовут Александр.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'My name is Alexander.' Russian has no 'my name is': it says 'me they call Alexander'. The 'they' is not spoken.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Выставка отменяется.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The exhibition is cancelled.' The event cancels itself, so ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "отменяется",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " reflects back. That's the Russian form for trains and events that are called off.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Передайте это Виктории.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Pass this on to Victoria.' The order is the polite one, ending in a special polite sound for ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Передайте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Виктории",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the 'to' form for the person it's passed to.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Хорошо, я передам.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'All right, I will pass it on.' We can analyze the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "передам",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'I will pass on'. It comes from the expression in Max's command, but now it's the future.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Граци, синьора, чао-чао.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Grazie, signora, ciao-ciao.' More fake Italian. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "чао",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is how Russians write 'ciao', and they use it as a casual goodbye with friends.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Да, алло. Виктория Сергеевна, здравствуйте.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, hello. Victoria Sergeyevna, hello.' Now let's analyze this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "алло",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is only for answering the phone. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "здравствуйте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " marks the polite hello for people you don't know well.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Звонил представитель выставки из Милана.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A representative of the exhibition from Milan called.' We will look at the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Звонил",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ends in a special sound for a man. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "из",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Милана",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'from Milan', with a change on the city after 'from'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Он сказал, что она отменяется.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He said that it is cancelled.' We can look at this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "она",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'she', because the term for trade show is feminine in Russian, so a trade show counts as a 'she'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "что",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " here means 'that'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "А, правда? Я уже в Домодедово.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, really? I am already in Domodedovo.' Now let's inspect this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "правда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'truth', and Russians say it to mean 'really?'. Vika is at one of Moscow's airports, and she says ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "в",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " for 'in' with no change to the name.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я чуть не улетела.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I almost flew away.' Let's examine this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "чуть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", almost, word-for-word means 'barely not'. Notice for 'flew off', ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "улетела",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", starts with a prefix for 'away'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо, что предупредили.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you for warning me.' 'Thanks that' is how you say thanks for something. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "предупредили",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", warned, ends in the form that goes with polite 'you'. Here it is, top to bottom.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Бонжорно, синьоры.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "angelina_h",
   "t": "Добрый день, ресторан Клод Моне.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
